# Working on this portfolio

This is a personal portfolio site built with Astro. The person you are helping
designed it and owns it, but is not a career engineer. Your job is to help them
keep building it quickly, confidently, and with as few errors as possible.

Deeper rules live in `.cursor/rules/` (design tokens, Astro conventions,
content, deploy). This file is the practical, day-to-day version.

## How to work with this person

- Explain what you are about to do in one or two plain sentences before you do
  it. Avoid jargon, or explain a term the first time you use it.
- Make one change at a time. Small changes are easy to understand, easy to
  check, and easy to undo if they are wrong.
- After a change, tell them how to see it: which page to open, what to look
  for. Assume the dev server is running (`npm run dev`) and updates live.
- If a step is risky or could break the site, say so first and keep it
  reversible. Prefer a branch for anything bigger than a small edit.
- Teach a little as you go, but keep it short. They want a working site, not a
  lecture.

## Keep the look consistent: use the design tokens

This is what stops the site from looking generic and makes big style changes
easy later. Every colour, size, font, and spacing value comes from a token in
`src/styles/tokens.css`.

- Use the semantic tokens: `--color-text`, `--color-bg`, `--color-border`,
  `--color-accent`, `--color-link`, `--font-body`, `--text-base`, `--space-md`,
  `--radius`, and so on. These are values with a job.
- Do not use the raw base tokens (`--gray-12`, `--accent-9`, `--size-3`,
  `--font-size-2`) directly, and never type a raw value like `#333`, `16px`,
  or a font name into a page or component.
- If nothing fits, add a new semantic token that points at a base value,
  rather than a one-off number.
- To restyle the site ("warmer", "bigger type", "more spacing"), change the
  tokens, not each page. One edit should move the whole site. That is the point.

## Astro basics that prevent errors

- Pages are files in `src/pages/`. The file path is the URL: `about.astro`
  becomes `/about`, `pages/work/index.astro` becomes `/work`. To add a page,
  add a file.
- An `.astro` file can start with a fenced section (`---` ... `---`) for code
  and imports, followed by the HTML. Keep both fences or the page will error.
- Reusable pieces (nav, footer, a card) go in `src/components/` and are
  imported into pages. Build a page as one file first, then pull the repeated
  parts into components once they repeat.
- Shared page shells (header + footer wrapping content) go in `src/layouts/`.
- Put images and files that should be served as-is in `public/`. Reference
  them from the site root, like `/images/photo.jpg`.
- After changing `astro.config.mjs` or installing something, restart the dev
  server so the change takes effect.

## Case studies and other repeating content

- Store repeating content (project case studies, posts) as Markdown/MDX files
  in `src/content/`, described by a schema in `src/content/config.ts`.
- Adding one is adding a new file, not pasting data into a page. Pages read the
  collection and render each entry through a component. Build the template
  once; every future entry is just another file.
- If you add a new field to an entry, add it to the schema too, so the site
  keeps checking entries for mistakes.
- Migrating from another platform: the user drops exports (CSV, old posts,
  images) into the top-level `content-source/` folder. Read from there and
  write proper content files into `src/content/`. `content-source/` is a
  staging area only and is never published.

## Keep it simple and fast

- This is a content site, not an app. Everything is built ahead of time and
  served as plain files. That is what makes it fast and cheap to host.
- Plain `.astro` files and CSS are enough for almost everything. Do not add
  React, Vue, Tailwind, or other libraries unless there is a real need and the
  user agrees. Each one is another thing to maintain and another way to break.
- Scoped `<style>` blocks inside `.astro` files are good for one-off styling.
  They should still use tokens.

## Saving and publishing

- The site is on GitHub and deploys to Vercel by itself. The loop is: edit,
  commit, push, and the live site updates in a minute or two.
- Keep commits small with a clear message of what changed. It makes it easy to
  find and undo a mistake later.
- Never commit passwords, API keys, or `.env` files. The `.gitignore` already
  excludes them; keep it that way.
- If a deploy fails, read the error at the top of the Vercel build log first.
  It is almost always a typo in a content file or a missing file.

## When unsure

- Ask before adding a dependency, deleting files, or changing many files at
  once.
- Match the patterns already in the project.
- Prefer the smallest change that solves the problem.
